        "id": 1157738
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-08-12T20:03:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "CMA memory is not marked reserved to start with, indeed it\u0027s part of the system memory. GCMA is a reserved memory, therefore once system wants to use it we should mark it unreserved. We can do that during GCMA init but I think that would be semantically incorrect. If we don\u0027t do that here then when we free the free_pages_prepare() will trigger a bad page report.",
      "parentUuid": "4a683a1b_4639cf3a",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 532,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 532,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "f02730b1221c8800dbfcd44d4eb7cadf210d73f0",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2c69d047_8c3a1e44",
        "filename": "mm/cma.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 532,
      "author": {
        "id": 1275554
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-08-12T20:33:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What\u0027s the different between CMA and GCMA?\n\nBoth can be used for driver on runtime and userspace when the driver owner is idle. Why does GCMA need to be handled differently? What semantic do you see the problem?\n\n\nThe other question: If we want to use PG_reserved flags dynamically, we need to review all the races cases. Have you checked it?",
      "parentUuid": "dbc4b4ad_d697cd63",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 532,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 532,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "f02730b1221c8800dbfcd44d4eb7cadf210d73f0",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "55cc4e0f_244de915",
        "filename": "mm/cma.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 532,
      "author": {
        "id": 1157738
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-08-12T20:49:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The conceptual difference in my mind is that CMA is part of the system memory. System can see it and can use it when needed. GCMA OTOH is a carveout that the System does not see until it requests it from GCMA.\n\nWhich races are you concerned? While the page is used by GCMA, it\u0027s marked as reserved and the system can\u0027t see these pages. Once system got the pages, they are not reserved and GCMA will not touch them. At which moment do you think there will be a race?",
      "parentUuid": "2c69d047_8c3a1e44",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 532,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 532,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "f02730b1221c8800dbfcd44d4eb7cadf210d73f0",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "247ea7b3_3136e216",
        "filename": "mm/cma.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 532,
      "author": {
        "id": 1275554
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-08-12T21:01:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "While you are changing the PG_reserved flags in this context, what happens if other CPU checks the PG_reserved flags in parallel? I didn\u0027t review cases and asking you checked that whether they are safe and not error prone in the future. \n\nFor the semantic, GCMA is something special compared to carveout so I don\u0027t have strong opinion wheather they should have PG_reserved or not.\nDo you see any problem if we remove the PG_reserved at GCMA init?",
      "parentUuid": "55cc4e0f_244de915",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 532,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 532,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "f02730b1221c8800dbfcd44d4eb7cadf210d73f0",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fef35b75_bec5c71d",
        "filename": "mm/cma.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 532,
      "author": {
        "id": 1157738
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-08-12T21:05:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "re: What happens if other CPU checks the PG_reserved flags in parallel?\n\nI don\u0027t think there should be concurrent checks for PG_reserved for a page the system already freed...\n\nre: Do you see any problem if we remove the PG_reserved at GCMA init?\n\nNo, not really. We should be able to do that and it\u0027s probably simpler compared to setting/clearing it every time we free/allocate. I\u0027ll change it that way and test.",
      "parentUuid": "247ea7b3_3136e216",
